6241286 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243857 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241328 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243857 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243857 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243857 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186243143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243857 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243857 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243857 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186243143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243857 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f269e409-7bf4-5c2e-903d-d263915d4aba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243874 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243874 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186241781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243874 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242518 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243874 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242580 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243874 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186242800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243874 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243874 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243874 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186241496 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186243874 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f91572ca-da1d-5596-9c9c-7fe17a660ab0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244231 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243672 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244231 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109112" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244231 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244231 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the rectangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(2,0),(2,1)$ and $(0,1),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ According to Green's theorem, \n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nCalculate the function $f(x,y).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244231 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c38c694f-327f-51b0-bb98-98b827fdfa0a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244231 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8d57fb7f6b8e2e822a11f11a0267994c449c37b43df167a8d48ab8036854b8dd.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{x}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(y\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{x}^{2}y)\\,dx+(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}[\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-{x}^{2})]\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-y\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)={x}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244233 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244233 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109115" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244233 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244233 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=(3xy-1)\\,i+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,j$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=2x$ and $y={x}^{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244233 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5f6efefc-16a2-55cb-8b63-0d3253a6815c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244233 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/5f/5f34ac74599937e0ae181126ff9a3da5b29dfe213614a1f23ca10c7062f22f7d.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3xy-1$ and $Q(x,y)=3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(3xy-1)\\,dx+(3{x}^{2}+{e}^{y})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(6x-3x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}3x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x[y]_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}3x(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(6{x}^{2}-3{x}^{3})\\,dx \\\\  & ={[2{x}^{3}-\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =2(2)^{3}-\\frac{3(2)^{4}}{4} \\\\  & =16-12 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244243 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243507 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244243 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244243 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244243 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y={x}^{2}-x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244243 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "13e80aba-bf74-59e1-8677-06e92124ae97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244243 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/e7/e788bbcbda4cc715c6989b7de9d08d60e3b0d178eeb9432a342a4c86c4c01b86.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2y-x$ and $Q(x,y)=5x-y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}-x\\le y\\le x\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2y-x)\\,dx+(5x-y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(5-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}[y]_{{x}^{2}-x}^{x}\\,dx \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,dx \\\\  & =3{[{x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =3(4-\\frac{8}{3}) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244244 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243501 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244244 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109164" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244244 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244244 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy,$ where $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and $y=\\sqrt{3x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244244 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d29c3a3a-1bb9-50bf-8673-d429ca766812" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244244 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/eb/eb0b120cc4d63c8dab18c8acb37f5835e99db746584262f6bb33e8ca99b6a16d.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2xy$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\le y\\le \\sqrt{3x}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}2xy\\,dx+{y}^{2}\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(0-2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}(-2x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)[y]_{{x}^{2}/3}^{\\sqrt{3x}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(-2x)(\\sqrt{3x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\sqrt{3}{x}^{3/2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})dx \\\\  & =-2{[\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\sqrt{3x}}{5}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{12}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{2(3)^{2}\\sqrt{3(3)}}{5}-\\frac{(3)^{4}}{12}) \\\\  & =-2(\\frac{54}{5}-\\frac{27}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244245 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244245 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109166" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244245 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244245 #edb/ref 1071 "Use Green's theorem to evaluate ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$ where $F(x,y)=⟨2x+{y}^{2},{x}^{2}+2y⟩$ and $C$ is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244245 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7203c090-0409-569d-a6e2-3e62f7801a90" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244245 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/fa/fafd281fc63fb276c9759fd84f722096d494aa3e3fe9441f43aa6ecd1f9dfda9.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}(2x+{y}^{2})\\,dx+({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}(2x-2y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[2xy-{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{{x}^{2}/3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}(\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{9})dx \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{x}^{4}}{6}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{45}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)^{4}}{6}-\\frac{(3)^{5}}{45} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{2}-\\frac{27}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{81}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244254 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186243857 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244254 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109249" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244254 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244254 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the closed curve $C,$ which is the boundary of the region enclosed by the curves $x=y$ and $x=4y-{y}^{2}.$ Express ${∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy$ as a repeated integral." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244254 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "806c47fd-68e8-5b88-97f5-76f2521ac858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186244254 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/23/239867aec8cff7840667d2f4eafa9bc97d4fc5af09b684d863fef06fd8fd28ad.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):y\\le x\\le 4y-{y}^{2},\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}({e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\,dx+(2x{e}^{y}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2{e}^{y}+2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-{e}^{y}-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{y}^{4y-{y}^{2}}{e}^{y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323987 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1-x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323987 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186323987 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "019501e8-6ea6-5368-99e8-cde9c215f7d2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324375 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324375 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324375 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "31bcb739-40d2-561c-b2d4-44bd167631a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324885 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324885 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324885 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6f3759c8-4733-51a4-abea-b8fa6dcbec97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325569 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{y}(1+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325569 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325569 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b9f9d8ec-7102-5b9e-a9d0-b789d1dc61fc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325911 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325911 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186325911 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "de42923d-c758-5be4-a046-273bd9c961bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186323987 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186324375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186324885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186325569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326348 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186325911 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326348 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326348 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326348 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186324885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326348 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4e66e1ab-6bec-5d89-bae1-cf35e17f31ed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326928 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186326348 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326928 #edb/ref 1069 "q-42107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326928 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326928 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,0),(0,-1)$ and $(1,0),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y⟩,$ as a repeated integral." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326928 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "977cc01a-2db6-51fc-a3f5-772fb936cc60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186326928 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d4f13abad5cc193dccedee3114fbd9cbd9fb8e6b5b3ddb0d07f0ed2ed51f4ff.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ and $Q(x,y)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le y+1,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{0}^{y+1}(1+x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\text{ }dx\\,dy\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386397 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186386398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386397 #edb/ref 1069 "q-109291" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386397 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386397 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the triangle $C$ in the plane with vertices $(0,-1),(1,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and the region $D$ bounded by $C.$ Express ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr,$  where $F(x,y)=⟨{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x⟩,$ as a repeated integral." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386397 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d02b88c9-898d-50c4-a59e-b0f309ffc0a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386397 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/c2/c2e08c233cb31124234ab6e5179998a3bdc148024632c7770bdea46cd37dff83.png)\n\nFirst, notice that we can rewrite our integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot dr={∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy\n$$\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,x-1\\le y\\le -2x+2\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's Theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}{y}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,dx+(xy-2y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(y+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{x-1}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386398 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386398 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386400 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386398 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386398 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386398 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186386403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386398 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386398 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386398 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186386401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386398 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "68b205c3-aadd-5002-89e3-15ef39ecf484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386399 #edb/ref 1080 "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{-2x+2}y\\text{ }dy\\,dx" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386399 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186386399 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0c0ab6a5-fedc-5172-9726-7c8b92e6d238" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
